 </tabular>
</table>
  <p>
     The keywords used in the HelloWorld program (<xref ref="lstng_helloworld"></xref>) are: 
    <c>class</c>,   <c>public</c>,    <c>static</c>, and
    <c>void</c>.
  </p>
  <p>
      Keywords cannot be used as the names of things (such as classes) in a program. Such a  
      <em>name</em> is called an <em>identifier</em>.
      The programmer can make up their own identifiers for the classes and 
      other program elements provided that this and other rules are followed.
  </p>
  <principle>
    <title>Identifier</title>
    <p>
        An <term>identifier</term> in Java must begin with a capital or lowercase letter and may be followed by any number of letters, digits,
        underscores (_), or dollar signs ($).
        An identifier may not be identical to a Java keyword.
    </p>
  </principle>
  <p>
      Names in Java are <em>case sensitive</em>,
      which means that two different identifiers may contain the same letters in the same order.
      For example, <c>thisVar</c> and <c>ThisVar</c> are two different identifiers.
  </p>
  <p>
      In addition to the syntax rule that governs identifiers, Java 
      programmers follow two <term>style conventions</term> when naming classes and
      other program elements that use identifiers. 
      Conventions make it is easier to distinguish the different elements in a program
      just by how they are written. (For more on Java style conventions, see Appendix A.).
      Since classes are our only example so far, the convention
      for class names is to begin with a capital letter and use capital letters to distinguish the individual
      words in the name. This is called <term>Camel-Case</term> For example, <c>HelloWorld</c>.
    </p>
<p>
      Another important style convention followed by Java programmers is to choose descriptive identifiers when naming classes
      and other program elements. This helps to make the program more readable. We'll talk more about this convention when we 
      get to <em>variable definitions</em>.
</p>
<principle>
<title>PROGRAMMING TIP: Choice of Identifiers</title>
<p>
      To make your program more readable,
      choose names that describe the purpose of the class, variable,
      or method.
</p>
</principle>
</subsection>
